  "before_edn": "{:db/id 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186064092 :question/id #uuid \"cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1\" :question/math-academy-id \"e-472\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle{\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt 2} \\\\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\\\, \\\\textrm{d}x}.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First, we rewrite the integral as\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int\\\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\\\,\\\\textrm d u = \\\\arctan{u} + C.\\n$$\\n\\nWe make the substitution $u=\\\\dfrac{x}{\\\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\dfrac{\\\\textrm d u}{\\\\textrm d x} = \\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt 2}\\\\quad\\\\Longrightarrow\\\\quad \\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,\\\\textrm d u = \\\\textrm d x.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\sqrt2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{1}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\cdot \\\\sqrt{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{2}}{8}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid \"f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6\" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048332 :topic/difficulty 1.7335518935867262 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id 17592186428117 :encompassing/rationale \"Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1159->1007. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json.\" :encompassing/topic {:db/id 17592186050271} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id 17592186428118 :encompassing/rationale \"Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foun