ned in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json.\" :encompassing/topic {:db/id 17592186049790} :encompassing/weight 0.3333333333333333} {:db/id 17592186428131 :encompassing/rationale \"Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1159->654. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json.\" :encompassing/topic {:db/id 17592186050764} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid \"95c6ac75-ad8e-5eab-9a14-9a2372fa3af7\" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id 17592186100874} {:db/id 17592186100877}] :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/next [{:db/id 17592186048115} {:db/id 17592186048150} {:db/id 17592186048189} {:db/id 17592186048403} {:db/id 17592186050101} {:db/id 17592186050299} {:db/id 17592186050489} {:db/id 17592186050898}] :topic/title \"Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution\"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186677202 :question-generator/id #uuid \"dfb22793-9205-5ab7-9f7c-cd3818ec6882\" :question-generator/script \"generators/dc/dc2c6e81c814b35a099aef56a40e934ddf374d633dd99d5b40e34f758270a5a0/generator.py\"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186064544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"400b63a0-4683-5a21-897e-9f816d13ecf4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186285387} {:answer/id #uuid \"72dc0745-c711-5555-b758-8857ea08db0a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186285845} {:answer/id #uuid \"7e97b556-fb4d-50ab-8db3-611fbf05e7d3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186285953} {:answer/id #uuid \"85326801-a074-5ff0-bebe-99064aa9feb0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186286012} {:answer/id #uuid \"b4f53bfe-9dac-5f4c-b151-f462bed9f133\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186286432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"72dc0745-c711-5555-b758-8857ea08db0a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186285845} :answer-field/id #uuid \"a839d901-bb07-5422-9156-0f0bc9d6308d\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"aa336cf9-b3b8-5d4a-a543-f9cee172da34\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19394\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\sqrt{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =2\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\\\\\  & =2\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\right)-2\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =2\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186064545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"3caebccd-a111-5c41-885b-601d0e3537ef\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\" :db/id 17592186285359} {:answer/id #uuid \"5c49b8ca-6a4c-5d5b-bee0-678ddd9d003c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(7)\" :db/id 17592186285633} {:answer/id #uuid \"77e0a940-8e6f-54a7-9506-301d4949f5ee\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(7)\" :db/id 17592186285904} {:answer/id #uuid \"c96c9e76-bbaf-5ac6-a64e-a08681842df9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\" :db/id 17592186286625} {:answer/id #uuid \"eda35d92-71e9-5870-9644-e45103f5e6c2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"9\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\" :db/id 17592186286967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"c96c9e76-bbaf-5ac6-a64e-a08681842df9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\" :db/id 17592186286625} :answer-field/id #uuid \"23ae6c57-6433-571b-8f19-8245bed72d97\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"4167c2cd-a204-5a1b-9ee4-1fca54336ae6\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19397\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{7}\\\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can rewrite this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{7}\\\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{9}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{7}\\\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=2\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $7$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $14$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{7}\\\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{9}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{7}\\\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{9}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{14}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{9}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{14}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{14} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)-\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{9}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186250184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"243323f6-ad70-573c-a08a-406469d3a0ed\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186247740} {:answer/id #uuid \"2f7538ae-df96-5bd6-89c4-eb9b39dfc0b8\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186247852} {:answer/id #uuid \"5bf8e15f-3174-57fb-b8e4-5d360ff846f4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{12}\" :db/id 17592186248257} {:answer/id #uuid \"9f8775a6-08c9-5e68-8ded-0ebea2805076\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"3\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186248846} {:answer/id #uuid \"a3358706-6bfa-540b-8aac-27669d842b97\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186248885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"5bf8e15f-3174-57fb-b8e4-5d360ff846f4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{12}\" :db/id 17592186248257} :answer-field/id #uuid \"6296fb51-a261-5315-98e2-bafc87eb4fd1\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"b4d360a8-fe79-5362-9972-fed2a1994303\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114074\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can rewrite this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(4x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=4\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{8}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{12}\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186250185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"3bd84a82-8cbc-585f-8bbe-ce19fdb09a44\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{8}\" :db/id 17592186247954} {:answer/id #uuid \"47871075-deb3-513b-8a8e-3639234f622c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186248060} {:answer/id #uuid \"983ebb92-bd83-5ed1-a0f3-8fe2c8c188ea\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186248782} {:answer/id #uuid \"a7c31917-04d7-582e-b1d4-e26865d402a0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186248917} {:answer/id #uuid \"af0b67fe-7271-56c8-8367-495b25ff2f54\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"8\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186248992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a7c31917-04d7-582e-b1d4-e26865d402a0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186248917} :answer-field/id #uuid \"25f9281c-e5d7-5f72-ac5c-0b25153c48f4\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249738}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"d8425d47-32c7-547c-ab87-ac1c6071e1e8\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114078\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/8)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/8}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{8})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{8}}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{8}}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\sqrt{8}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{8})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (\\\\sqrt{8}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186250186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"012323fc-4e7b-5948-bd33-ac6535cdacea\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186247423} {:answer/id #uuid \"275108dc-ba21-516f-8c42-bd8bf8d71064\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{8}\" :db/id 17592186247772} {:answer/id #uuid \"3ab5218b-e36e-59ff-8b03-f71a3f5c4374\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{24}\" :db/id 17592186247944} {:answer/id #uuid \"82b6c265-2f3d-5dbe-b622-87847517b9ff\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{8}\" :db/id 17592186248580} {:answer/id #uuid \"afcf5a5d-6b1c-5e07-b91a-41eca607a863\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{8\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186249002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"afcf5a5d-6b1c-5e07-b91a-41eca607a863\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{8\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186249002} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f5f84de4-1a81-5ce2-ac8e-5901c4a2a352\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186250104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"c216ad6d-32f4-5235-ae6f-6f4e1e6db054\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114080\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{8}{\\\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{8}{\\\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =8{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{4\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{8}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =4{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{2}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=2\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\sqrt{3}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{8}{\\\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =4{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =4{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (2\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =8{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =8\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\\\\\  & =8(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =8(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{8\\\\pi }{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186250187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"01b573ef-1007-53ad-a1f4-f1bbb6a97f3a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{9}\" :db/id 17592186247434} {:answer/id #uuid \"21345a50-5601-54ac-b6b8-8854f3bcf25d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186247719} {:answer/id #uuid \"21932059-2779-56bb-aff8-46d2133806aa\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186247722} {:answer/id #uuid \"b2ca1c00-7abb-53af-b061-a218e5f95af2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186249025} {:answer/id #uuid \"fe379fad-f57a-50e1-ad28-7deffbfbf656\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{9}\" :db/id 17592186249647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"01b573ef-1007-53ad-a1f4-f1bbb6a97f3a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{9}\" :db/id 17592186247434} :answer-field/id #uuid \"20529d1c-5c66-5379-87b8-bf5bcb3ca23c\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"d206bd2a-2b8b-589e-945a-46c1dd7ed6d1\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114084\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{3\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{2}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/3}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/3}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{9}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186287944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"07df5620-8117-5d74-b65a-568506c2ddda\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186284848} {:answer/id #uuid \"4699fac1-5a8f-5b40-afed-7064158b1c33\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186285437} {:answer/id #uuid \"9ce9442e-a29f-597e-b2de-7761a1d30d17\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186286222} {:answer/id #uuid \"a22f372f-9504-584b-bc3e-87c16eaabc46\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186286274} {:answer/id #uuid \"e584a0a9-3825-5a55-8fc4-911388ec2a92\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186286888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a22f372f-9504-584b-bc3e-87c16eaabc46\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186286274} :answer-field/id #uuid \"2ff436e5-f03c-513f-b406-812caeb45b91\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"25b26ce0-b29c-55b2-a912-d3113c434dd9\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19393\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{5}{\\\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{5}{\\\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =5{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{16\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{4}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=4\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{5}{\\\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{5}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (4\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =5{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =5\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =5\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-5\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{6}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186287945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0bbb1cae-6e39-530c-8350-eb936f667815\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186284882} {:answer/id #uuid \"5ee39338-b490-52d5-8051-37da3e5e957f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186285657} {:answer/id #uuid \"64faa062-9068-565a-bb31-39dbd6d07913\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186285705} {:answer/id #uuid \"68959faf-15c8-528e-bc75-90d2d2a0e5cc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186285749} {:answer/id #uuid \"f7445989-6f9b-5004-8c4b-e95fea2ee953\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186287053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"68959faf-15c8-528e-bc75-90d2d2a0e5cc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186285749} :answer-field/id #uuid \"77436f8b-d0d6-52c6-99f0-ade76c2dfb27\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"20d8d0f4-d57e-564e-be24-7116faf35cef\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19395\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =3{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3\\\\cdot (1-4{x}^{2})}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3}}{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n  \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(2x)}^{2}.$   \\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=2\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $-0.25$ | $0.25$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $-0.5$ | $0.5$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\\\int }_{-0.5}^{0.5}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-0.5}^{0.5} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.5)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.5)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{6}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186287946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"331c449c-78bb-5f8b-9dcd-69e5f627c3c7\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186285253} {:answer/id #uuid \"53cbca06-5dff-57fe-91ef-daa24c5147f0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186285559} {:answer/id #uuid \"7d34e51c-e8eb-58a3-86e7-2325ea4401f5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186285940} {:answer/id #uuid \"c0230150-a2bf-5bf4-a26a-c06c4bd66ae0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186286545} {:answer/id #uuid \"db135799-7845-5945-af89-06c54826a98a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186286784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"c0230150-a2bf-5bf4-a26a-c06c4bd66ae0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186286545} :answer-field/id #uuid \"b6de2d23-7e6a-5191-bba5-d88af28f962e\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186287530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"0d25ee70-cfd4-55fa-9b35-c615d86700ba\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19396\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can write this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{20}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=5\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{5}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{20}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\\\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{20}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186319103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"e406c8b4-fe2c-53cd-abdc-dc32a0608267\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186318327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"e406c8b4-fe2c-53cd-abdc-dc32a0608267\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186318327} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c443a9b7-8c24-5662-be21-7a311e519118\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186318847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"0ba9633b-5871-51f9-8aee-a3691915151d\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-346451\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=3\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{3}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\\\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{3}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186319105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"a18383d3-63cd-5639-931d-a7a1c504ecad\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186318118} {:answer/id #uuid \"eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186527772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"eef5da2c-2eb9-5619-b5fc-8c0b83845f3e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186527772} :answer-field/id #uuid \"d4db02f7-5977-595a-8560-ebd6520e3bc1\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186318876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"684a7fc3-f685-5be8-90e1-52ef15ddd822\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-346458\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =6{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{100\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/100)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{6}{10}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/100}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{3}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{10}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{10}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{10}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=10\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{100-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{3}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/10)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{3}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot 10\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =6{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =6\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =6\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-6\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =6\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\pi \\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2a7bafaa-3cbb-533d-b844-15cf79b3fe34\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{5}}{12}\" :db/id 17592186319530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"2a7bafaa-3cbb-533d-b844-15cf79b3fe34\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{5}}{12}\" :db/id 17592186319530} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f18bc040-ce10-594f-bb02-f617ff11efb9\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"b439af01-6063-5a0d-8dfc-d235cf8c0346\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351291\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{10}{\\\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{10}{\\\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =10{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{5(1-16{x}^{2})}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{\\\\sqrt{5}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =2\\\\sqrt{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine, \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(4x)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=4\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{8}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{10}{\\\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =2\\\\sqrt{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =2\\\\sqrt{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{5}}{2}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{5}}{12}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"a6d4b36a-43a6-585a-a12c-95ad82f0ab96\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"5\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186319889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a6d4b36a-43a6-585a-a12c-95ad82f0ab96\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"5\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186319889} :answer-field/id #uuid \"1307c487-f3d6-5566-a68c-5623882801fd\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"b0098fa7-60fc-5090-aeb3-d82239a596c1\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351827\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =15{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{36\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/36)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{15}{6}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/36}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{6}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{6}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=6\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3\\\\sqrt{3}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{5}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/6)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot 6\\\\,\\\\text{d}u \\\\\\\\  & =15{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =15\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\\\,{|}_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\\\\\  & =15\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\right)-15\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =15\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3} \\\\\\\\  & =5\\\\pi \\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"c4d6374a-a8f4-5012-ba14-79979bb09a1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\pi }{5}\" :db/id 17592186319994} {:answer/id #uuid \"1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}\" :db/id 17592186537260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}\" :db/id 17592186537260} :answer-field/id #uuid \"d0e46496-0573-5f7f-b6a6-15d10b4e8306\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320599}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"88256796-f1d7-5511-b30e-ef033cdfdfae\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351834\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =3{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3\\\\cdot (1-25{x}^{2})}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3}}{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n  \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(5x)}^{2}.$   \\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=5\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $-0.2$ | $0.2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\sqrt{3}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\\\,\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,\\\\pi }{5}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"a220c465-cd23-556a-b6f2-70e7e4ee705c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{18}\" :db/id 17592186319874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a220c465-cd23-556a-b6f2-70e7e4ee705c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{18}\" :db/id 17592186319874} :answer-field/id #uuid \"3f065e5b-1a3e-5164-964f-ea2efbce87b8\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320306}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"8a43d62d-e653-5802-9217-17e3b89ace8c\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351835\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can rewrite this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(6x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=6\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{6}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{12}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =2{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{6}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{3}(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{18}\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"5ef55d3a-1d64-5e75-a936-57964a2c5268\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4}{3}\" :db/id 17592186319675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"5ef55d3a-1d64-5e75-a936-57964a2c5268\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4}{3}\" :db/id 17592186319675} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c611600a-1f7e-5aff-a691-ea5100dd312b\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"a866bed5-d733-533a-9199-2b822335e2cd\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"12\" :db/id 17592186319897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a866bed5-d733-533a-9199-2b822335e2cd\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"12\" :db/id 17592186319897} :answer-field/id #uuid \"cf733acb-7ff6-5434-afab-1202dfb73dee\" :answer-field/key \"field-2\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"474d3d58-1cc2-50c1-8314-228946c22d89\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351839\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{4}\\\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\\n$\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\\n{{field-2}}$)$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can rewrite this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{4}\\\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=4{\\\\int }_{0}^{4}\\\\frac{1}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=3\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $12$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4}\\\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =4{\\\\int }_{0}^{4}\\\\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =4{\\\\int }_{0}^{12}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{12}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{12} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(12)-\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\,arctan\\\\,(12)\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186320920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"3e28a0da-0d28-58b0-9ea9-a556621c9cc4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\" :db/id 17592186319579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3e28a0da-0d28-58b0-9ea9-a556621c9cc4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\" :db/id 17592186319579} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f17d058d-f41c-502a-aa59-824f88279a31\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186320671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"6c01a93a-0fc6-5d70-b6e3-096c747b914e\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351855\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}$)$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/12)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/12}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{12})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{12}}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{12}}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12}}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\sqrt{12}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/\\\\sqrt{12})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{12}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (\\\\sqrt{12}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{1/\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\right)\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186332736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"24d0c2f0-e1ef-5f9d-b277-1d69053a1f8c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186330265} {:answer/id #uuid \"5d04e998-1dc4-5619-8be2-aa1e5c1b7c5b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186330779} {:answer/id #uuid \"866ac174-f329-56ce-b375-426c38e8089c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)\" :db/id 17592186331143} {:answer/id #uuid \"a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186331438} {:answer/id #uuid \"d944c1b0-1445-5a57-b346-1c4580f6e918\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186331877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186331438} :answer-field/id #uuid \"7066a7e5-a4e7-571e-8102-812757ff24f9\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186332406}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"bf21744c-e558-571b-b3ed-9b3f13019f23\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-49538\" :question/problem \"What is the value of $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\,?\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=2\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}x}{1+(2x)^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186332737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"46e86a4b-cc4c-5a6f-aaf3-ef2234d941ed\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186330588} {:answer/id #uuid \"569e6b60-bf68-55c0-a58d-f5683e2d1476\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186330724} {:answer/id #uuid \"87600d1e-7d95-5096-a855-c3bc9ca0e904\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186331159} {:answer/id #uuid \"9e6b1507-21a9-5241-8a35-d41f437e8d29\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186331368} {:answer/id #uuid \"b73d0144-5e6d-5adf-89c2-88b202a8b0e0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186331568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"b73d0144-5e6d-5adf-89c2-88b202a8b0e0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186331568} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ece6c44b-e4c3-57ea-8f7c-8273c4e5c5c4\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186332617}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"edefe020-e961-5a43-95be-73f3210a7870\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-49546\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}x}{\\\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}x}{\\\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\\\, & ={\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{25\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{5}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=5\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $-\\\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\\\frac{5}{2}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $-\\\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}x}{\\\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}} & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (5\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(-\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186368428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"65f4d436-59f3-5763-bcdc-b3dd57872260\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{5}\" :db/id 17592186368430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"65f4d436-59f3-5763-bcdc-b3dd57872260\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{5}\" :db/id 17592186368430} :answer-field/id #uuid \"d6eaf7b2-0903-5d1f-bf6c-e8f7966021aa\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186368429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"29fd1abb-98ff-5074-9886-b94701e8a3c3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"5\" :db/id 17592186368432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"29fd1abb-98ff-5074-9886-b94701e8a3c3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"5\" :db/id 17592186368432} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ef16f665-49b9-5951-8bf5-581178032400\" :answer-field/key \"field-2\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186368431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"3aa951d0-f5d2-580d-8cda-935baa717731\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351850\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\\n$\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\\n{{field-2}}$)$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{25\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/25)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{25}{\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/25}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{25}{\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{5}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=5\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $25$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $5$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{1}{25}{\\\\int }_{0}^{25}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{25}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (5\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{5}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{5} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\,arctan\\\\,(5)\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186377538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"d90c6aca-5f52-581d-b9b7-53525c39db0b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186377540} {:answer/id #uuid \"35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{6}\" :db/id 17592186427994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{6}\" :db/id 17592186427994} :answer-field/id #uuid \"8d0f7cff-e86b-56b3-9a00-cbb02d98343e\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186377539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"4068a5d2-2d7d-5115-9e90-ed45feea5f4e\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351845\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{10}{9\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{9}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{9}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{3}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=3\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{10}{9}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{9}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (3\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\frac{10}{3}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{10}{3}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{5\\\\pi }{6}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186428007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186427993} {:answer/id #uuid \"83a4d996-c213-5d67-946a-4cd6fe97edb9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186427998} {:answer/id #uuid \"abc0b7d1-6dfb-5790-a262-2aa331de3a0a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186427999} {:answer/id #uuid \"bb2aeebc-c3cc-5f32-bec9-7d9027991243\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}\" :db/id 17592186428000} {:answer/id #uuid \"ea5fd737-b612-553b-8e19-91de7f399fb5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2\\\\pi \" :db/id 17592186428002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186427993} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ae824470-993a-5443-bdc9-a07993674d20\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186428005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"bacbcea3-6ad6-5840-9bf7-1e8371e0974d\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114075\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =3{\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{9\\\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/9)}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =3\\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/9}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n  \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{3}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=3\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{3}{2}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\cdot (3\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =3{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =3\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =3(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =3(\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186537232} {:answer/id #uuid \"0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{20}\" :db/id 17592186537250} {:answer/id #uuid \"7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{5}\" :db/id 17592186537477} {:answer/id #uuid \"b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4\\\\pi }{5}\" :db/id 17592186537565} {:answer/id #uuid \"c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186537616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{20}\" :db/id 17592186537250} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-114073\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can write this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{2}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=2\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{2}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{2}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{20}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186580948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2b5a38d2-5ac3-59e4-b944-d3d0d9cc05e4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186580457} {:answer/id #uuid \"4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186580522} {:answer/id #uuid \"60405895-e035-58cb-842d-10b09a8a4932\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)\" :db/id 17592186580549} {:answer/id #uuid \"bf692bc8-d65d-5ceb-9734-cda57421cb3e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)\" :db/id 17592186580691} {:answer/id #uuid \"dab99a72-47a9-51d7-b3b5-d9c4ea5ed620\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)\" :db/id 17592186580729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\" :db/id 17592186580522} :answer-field/id #uuid \"016466b0-e060-5aab-b591-d71625582e46\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186580789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"c579c1f3-be8e-5aac-8600-10eab58ffbc7\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-49549\" :question/problem \"$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{16\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{16}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{16}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{4}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=4\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $8$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{1}{16}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{16}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (4\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{4}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{1}{4}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186608164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"1eb2f314-6218-5d20-846d-b1f62c244921\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186607839} {:answer/id #uuid \"1f4d7f16-9e28-5cde-9ed7-301d3003cbf3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186607841} {:answer/id #uuid \"4b379efb-0a72-500a-8ebf-e235cb2af673\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{10}\" :db/id 17592186607901} {:answer/id #uuid \"bafabf47-e243-501e-acbd-67f3d795684d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186608015} {:answer/id #uuid \"fdc1e9c9-25e8-5c9a-a2fb-c81db3158f1d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{3}\" :db/id 17592186608088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"bafabf47-e243-501e-acbd-67f3d795684d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\" :db/id 17592186608015} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f92ab846-6ecb-5402-adcb-73ad9f5b7d61\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186608163}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"08c4a96f-48cd-5998-9cd6-61ef07570afd\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-49536\" :question/problem \"Find the value of $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Note that we can rewrite this integral as\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=15{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(5x)^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=5\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{10}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\\\frac{15}{\\\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =15{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =15{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\cdot (\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =3{\\\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\\\\\  & =3\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\\\\  & =3\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)-3\\\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186608213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"a4905c93-59e4-5489-bd39-01a851d21814\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{5}}\" :db/id 17592186608000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a4905c93-59e4-5489-bd39-01a851d21814\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{5}}\" :db/id 17592186608000} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c0dee006-775c-5be5-9366-3859c81c1fc2\" :answer-field/key \"field-1\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186608148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"a9da4d62-79c8-546a-8cfa-f2f42789b365\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-351854\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\n$$\\n{{field-1}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{4}{5\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/5)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/5}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{\\\\sqrt{5}}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=\\\\sqrt{5}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\\\sqrt{5}$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{4}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (\\\\sqrt{5}\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4\\\\sqrt{5}}{5}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4\\\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4\\\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{4\\\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{5}\\\\,\\\\pi }{5}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186608217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"10252354-59e0-52a9-9b48-74493655f2f4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7\\\\pi }{8}\" :db/id 17592186607823} {:answer/id #uuid \"14ee31c6-4603-5df0-8b74-6be6e55eac1c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7\\\\pi }{16}\" :db/id 17592186607828} {:answer/id #uuid \"4f98b232-1df5-5b21-af96-8e7b1948133d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7\\\\pi }{4}\" :db/id 17592186607909} {:answer/id #uuid \"71d9a123-911c-547c-8814-a2abefd2a870\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{49\\\\pi }{20}\" :db/id 17592186607941} {:answer/id #uuid \"eb6848f6-e949-5df8-aef3-41b88ea3c1cf\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{49\\\\pi }{80}\" :db/id 17592186608062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"10252354-59e0-52a9-9b48-74493655f2f4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7\\\\pi }{8}\" :db/id 17592186607823} :answer-field/id #uuid \"4ba83c09-1b1f-54ba-93e5-b8d879de48d7\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186608108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"b356033c-192a-55fa-8af3-a9a824c7a21b\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-19398\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x.\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{7}{4\\\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\text{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\\n$$\\n\\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\\\left(\\\\frac{x}{2}\\\\right)}^{2}.$\\n\\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{\\\\text{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,⟹\\\\,\\\\text{d}x=2\\\\,\\\\text{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\\n\\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\\n\\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x & =\\\\frac{7}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}x \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{4}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2}\\\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\\\cdot (2\\\\,\\\\text{d}u) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}\\\\frac{\\\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\\\frac{7}{2}\\\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7}{2}\\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4} \\\\\\\\  & =\\\\frac{7\\\\pi }{8}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0a19f002-f394-581c-8a71-944f99d29f1c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{360}\" :db/id 17592186677049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"0a19f002-f394-581c-8a71-944f99d29f1c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{360}\" :db/id 17592186677049} :answer-field/id #uuid \"b9486294-79c8-509d-84d4-b54229d994a1\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"0372c2fd-66f2-541c-882f-7c71be7cc96e\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{10 x^{2} + 9}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n10 x^{2} + 9=9(1+(\\\\frac{10}{9})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{3}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{3},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{3})(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10})=1 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{30}}{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{3})(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{30}}{10})=\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{10 x^{2} + 9}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{9}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{3})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{9}(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\\\int_{1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{30}\\\\int_{1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{30}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{30}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\sqrt{3})-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{30}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{360}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"891d5a4a-d8d5-502c-9dfc-2c65f90cc8ec\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{5}\" :db/id 17592186677112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"891d5a4a-d8d5-502c-9dfc-2c65f90cc8ec\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{5}\" :db/id 17592186677112} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5a9796b7-f543-5dc8-b952-8b55e73eb6a2\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677097}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"21a4ebc8-c316-5cfd-93e4-ca1118604531\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{10}} \\\\frac{8}{x^{2} + 10}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\nx^{2} + 10=10(1+(\\\\frac{1}{10})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{10}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\sqrt{10})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\sqrt{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10})(\\\\sqrt{10})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{10}} \\\\frac{8}{x^{2} + 10}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{10}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{10})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}(\\\\sqrt{10})\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{5}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"093ea6db-39b4-540d-abdb-9f769f0d4190\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}\" :db/id 17592186677046} {:answer/id #uuid \"297649ef-44df-588e-b84f-acc835a9587d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{49}\" :db/id 17592186677068} {:answer/id #uuid \"3658debc-c4b0-50eb-b768-7e1015c693c4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{7}\" :db/id 17592186677078} {:answer/id #uuid \"59ced480-f5f7-5e7c-b714-da8fe15e0eec\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{36 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{7}\" :db/id 17592186677096} {:answer/id #uuid \"dee94e97-9107-57cf-8abb-af0261012ca6\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{7}\" :db/id 17592186677177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3658debc-c4b0-50eb-b768-7e1015c693c4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{7}\" :db/id 17592186677078} :answer-field/id #uuid \"39a7f2e1-904a-5576-a7b2-3a71b7ec4a32\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"24b61c76-ca28-5b5c-9a6e-51d22a7f675a\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{14}} \\\\frac{6}{49 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{7})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(7)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{14}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(7)(\\\\frac{1}{14})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{14}} \\\\frac{6}{49 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 6\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{14}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(7 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 6(\\\\frac{1}{7})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}((\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{7}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"dad5e5d4-f28a-5e3c-9d7d-2ef281b00081\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{36}\" :db/id 17592186677173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"dad5e5d4-f28a-5e3c-9d7d-2ef281b00081\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{36}\" :db/id 17592186677173} :answer-field/id #uuid \"0970acb4-17e3-5133-8c74-81024d88697a\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"26a5ab0e-8ab0-5f06-8396-7feffadcde08\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{16(1-(\\\\frac{3}{8})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{4}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{6}}{3})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4})(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})=\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4})(\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{4})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}(\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{6}}{3})\\\\int_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{36}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"8fe824ad-db88-5a89-a4f6-708549ce42b2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677121} {:answer/id #uuid \"a7d547bc-cbd1-53b4-84fe-a0b07f98b84d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{144}\" :db/id 17592186677132} {:answer/id #uuid \"b2bb5d3e-7051-5abd-89a8-2b656aebf919\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\pi}{36}\" :db/id 17592186677136} {:answer/id #uuid \"c87fae49-0f53-5df6-911f-e8c7a1db7141\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{48}\" :db/id 17592186677156} {:answer/id #uuid \"f4ca0d1e-aed4-5152-9954-1989b7c30009\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"8fe824ad-db88-5a89-a4f6-708549ce42b2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677121} :answer-field/id #uuid \"943865ee-77d8-59f0-8739-263d0db8cbb9\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677123}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"275ef93a-9e15-50cd-b072-9c822daf3676\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}} \\\\frac{2}{6 x^{2} + 4}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n6 x^{2} + 4=4(1+(\\\\frac{3}{2})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{2}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2})(- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{3})=- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2})(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}} \\\\frac{2}{6 x^{2} + 4}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{3}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{2})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"66ab3c07-aed9-5048-be41-aa6b32baf7e9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{10}\" :db/id 17592186677100} {:answer/id #uuid \"b2fb651a-cb3e-5812-8920-65d6a708aba9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{5}\" :db/id 17592186677137} {:answer/id #uuid \"e3d3fca2-2f4b-5bb9-9152-d118b4c0a190\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677184} {:answer/id #uuid \"f6f4d4a4-75e2-54f0-a2b8-e12175ae170b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{10}\" :db/id 17592186677194} {:answer/id #uuid \"fd4d363a-e4b7-53bf-85fa-46bfd12b2ff3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{12 \\\\pi}{5}\" :db/id 17592186677198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"f6f4d4a4-75e2-54f0-a2b8-e12175ae170b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{10}\" :db/id 17592186677194} :answer-field/id #uuid \"15535d36-569d-5a66-912f-4328e0b08515\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"31069773-e2d9-58b9-845a-c488f05a3fd1\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{30}}{5}} \\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{64 - 10 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{64 - 10 x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{64(1-(\\\\frac{5}{32})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{8}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{8},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{8})(- \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5})=- \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{30}}{5}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{8})(\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{30}}{5})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{30}}{5}} \\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{64 - 10 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}}^{\\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{30}}{5}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{8})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}(\\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5})\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(- \\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{10}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"00551039-d607-5600-acd4-4dc4aef60bd8\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\pi}{6}\" :db/id 17592186677042} {:answer/id #uuid \"b6d5e3ae-feb8-5269-8089-cd58f1844709\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677143} {:answer/id #uuid \"cc4e4223-fd94-5f6b-a213-4216edf8b5c0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677162} {:answer/id #uuid \"e2796685-73b6-5e94-ae79-b8111f678b61\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677182} {:answer/id #uuid \"e31ba5e2-04d8-54e5-a488-c8bdb83fb3ba\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"e31ba5e2-04d8-54e5-a488-c8bdb83fb3ba\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677183} :answer-field/id #uuid \"fdb23a87-6b16-5bae-8d50-4399319e99f9\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"3678de14-c7a3-5309-a87e-827f356a473e\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{2 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{2(1-(\\\\frac{1}{2})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2} x}{2}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\sqrt{2})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{2 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\sqrt{2}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2} x}{2})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\sqrt{2}(\\\\sqrt{2})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 2\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 2[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 2(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= 2((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"07b08bd5-31bc-5c5a-ad01-1f39fa210754\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677045} {:answer/id #uuid \"27f548bb-c49c-5514-862f-6732a1a79088\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10} \\\\left(\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)} + \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\right)}\\\\right)}{5}\" :db/id 17592186677067} {:answer/id #uuid \"35e36aac-75f3-5dc4-9fb4-3e6fa1d1db11\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"3 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677077} {:answer/id #uuid \"cbf674a6-5283-5813-b916-60b747375328\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677161} {:answer/id #uuid \"fd2d7aad-0c09-5ccd-bdf2-b741e87a396f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{10 \\\\pi}{27}\" :db/id 17592186677197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"cbf674a6-5283-5813-b916-60b747375328\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677161} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c2f4f8ce-ed94-551d-89d9-8b2e56b37b4a\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"4a24f09e-263b-5efe-ac71-a976bc84e3aa\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{20}}^{\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{5}}{10}} \\\\frac{8}{\\\\sqrt{81 - 10 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{81 - 10 x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{81(1-(\\\\frac{10}{81})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{9}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{9},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{20}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{9})(- \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{20})=- \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{5}}{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{9})(\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{5}}{10})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{20}}^{\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{5}}{10}} \\\\frac{8}{\\\\sqrt{81 - 10 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{8}{9}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{20}}^{\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{5}}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{9})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{8}{9}(\\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(- \\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} \\\\pi}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"420aac5e-015a-5dcf-96c2-aa80b9e6f176\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{140}\" :db/id 17592186677087} {:answer/id #uuid \"8e4169e3-9262-5493-b315-2902bc48450b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{500}\" :db/id 17592186677118} {:answer/id #uuid \"8f897da0-c7c7-5ad7-bec0-4f2425e87fbe\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{140}\" :db/id 17592186677119} {:answer/id #uuid \"c74a561c-863a-573f-9341-b53ede55d47d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{28}\" :db/id 17592186677154} {:answer/id #uuid \"f5aeea5c-ba27-5f18-85d7-6f9fc18ce357\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{420}\" :db/id 17592186677193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"8f897da0-c7c7-5ad7-bec0-4f2425e87fbe\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{140}\" :db/id 17592186677119} :answer-field/id #uuid \"76e776ca-23c1-5993-b4b0-77109e7c5373\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"4eddb1ed-78dc-5c0e-a58a-4f6c59d0a7cf\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\\\frac{9}{7 x^{2} + 25}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n7 x^{2} + 25=25(1+(\\\\frac{7}{25})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7} x}{5}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7}}{5},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{21}}{21}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7}}{5})(\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{21}}{21})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7}}{5})(\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\\\frac{9}{7 x^{2} + 25}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{25}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{7} x}{5})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{25}(\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{7}}{35}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{7}}{35}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{7}}{35}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9 \\\\sqrt{7}}{35}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{7} \\\\pi}{140}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"cf36209d-dd8d-57a1-a765-f750e1391b98\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"cf36209d-dd8d-57a1-a765-f750e1391b98\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677167} :answer-field/id #uuid \"b352b4d8-ef8e-542f-94af-6fac013fa473\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677138}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"561f8902-181d-50e5-8116-48d2cb1ddaae\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}} \\\\frac{4}{\\\\sqrt{10 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{10 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{10(1-(\\\\frac{1}{10})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{10}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\sqrt{10})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\sqrt{5}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10}}{10})(\\\\sqrt{5})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}} \\\\frac{4}{\\\\sqrt{10 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{5}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{10} x}{10})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\\\sqrt{10})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 4\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 4[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 4(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= 4((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\pi\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"fdc0d3f5-a51b-5597-9b0f-2b50228b93a1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{54}\" :db/id 17592186677200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"fdc0d3f5-a51b-5597-9b0f-2b50228b93a1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{54}\" :db/id 17592186677200} :answer-field/id #uuid \"d1b888f5-5707-53a5-b970-7313bcf41529\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677168}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"5880c807-c10d-50e8-9d88-c434c7630fa9\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- 3 \\\\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\\\frac{6}{3 x^{2} + 81}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n3 x^{2} + 81=81(1+(\\\\frac{1}{27})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} x}{9}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{9},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(3 \\\\sqrt{3})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- 3 \\\\sqrt{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{9})(- 3 \\\\sqrt{3})=-1 \\\\\\\\ x=9&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{9})(9)=\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- 3 \\\\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\\\frac{6}{3 x^{2} + 81}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{27}\\\\int_{- 3 \\\\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} x}{9})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{27}(3 \\\\sqrt{3})\\\\int_{-1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\\\int_{-1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{9}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{-1}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{9}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\sqrt{3})-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(-1)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}{9}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7 \\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{54}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"04ac5bda-af2a-567a-972e-4eaed79b2cb5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{100}\" :db/id 17592186677044} {:answer/id #uuid \"a63b8559-c82f-598b-a90b-350bd66e1c91\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{20}\" :db/id 17592186677129} {:answer/id #uuid \"a864f77b-e61b-569b-b091-f06fcbea3c77\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677133} {:answer/id #uuid \"ae1a1d8e-c665-5fe6-a286-59bed10b0dc3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\right)}\" :db/id 17592186677135} {:answer/id #uuid \"daced14b-3627-5e01-8e59-8270db580180\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a864f77b-e61b-569b-b091-f06fcbea3c77\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677133} :answer-field/id #uuid \"bd293653-c045-569e-88f6-fbf74712702f\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"682222db-91f6-5331-b0ae-5361f8ce335b\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{25(1-(\\\\frac{1}{25})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{5}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{5},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(5)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{5})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{2}}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{5})(\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{x}{5})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{5}(5)\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 1\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 1[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 1(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= 1((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"35733d7a-08b3-5eb1-b623-b285be791351\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{9 \\\\pi}{32}\" :db/id 17592186677076} {:answer/id #uuid \"bcc765d0-19d2-52d4-8cae-2b1ce27c3960\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{32}\" :db/id 17592186677149} {:answer/id #uuid \"caff1140-af84-5fee-a20b-46dfa8dfd943\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9 \\\\pi}{32}\" :db/id 17592186677159} {:answer/id #uuid \"cbd0e262-e697-5db3-b587-5ac8bdb7b7be\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9 \\\\pi}{256}\" :db/id 17592186677160} {:answer/id #uuid \"dfafb482-d854-543e-90ed-7b48a0404ff0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"18 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"caff1140-af84-5fee-a20b-46dfa8dfd943\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{9 \\\\pi}{32}\" :db/id 17592186677159} :answer-field/id #uuid \"234d093f-86d5-5259-9076-fc2ff176a74e\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677062}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"6871a209-fee3-5a51-a701-ff2bbca02c0a\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\frac{9}{64 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{8})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{8}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(\\\\frac{1}{8})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\frac{9}{64 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 9\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(8 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 9(\\\\frac{1}{8})\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{8}\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{8}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{8}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9}{8}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{9 \\\\pi}{32}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"10b98030-d11f-5f99-938a-5366d17d6d1d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{4 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677052} {:answer/id #uuid \"2984adf6-e7b5-5a90-8825-5cdf2f26581a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677069} {:answer/id #uuid \"356b89e1-d725-57e8-b073-eb5591c2bcc3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"8 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}\" :db/id 17592186677075} {:answer/id #uuid \"9d065717-78b1-5e32-873d-9ec56ea739b4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\right)}}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677124} {:answer/id #uuid \"fc523c34-26f5-5ee8-9c58-6eb37bbddad6\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"48 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}\" :db/id 17592186677196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"2984adf6-e7b5-5a90-8825-5cdf2f26581a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677069} :answer-field/id #uuid \"708a5c04-84ae-5fc7-8d2f-1654bd8d1621\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677103}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"7f635271-ad06-57ec-ba14-197476f846bc\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{8}{36 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{6})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(6)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(6)(\\\\frac{1}{2})=3\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{8}{36 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 8\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(6 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 8(\\\\frac{1}{6})\\\\int_{0}^{3} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{3} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(3)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}((\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(3 \\\\right)}}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2ed763b1-4372-543e-8ee2-dcc84046626c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{18}\" :db/id 17592186677072} {:answer/id #uuid \"755143cd-5ed9-5e1b-8d5b-5cda84267c28\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{6}\" :db/id 17592186677104} {:answer/id #uuid \"e43f83e7-80cb-56df-b32b-9031973aa5a3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677186} {:answer/id #uuid \"ea5e3f07-99ff-540a-9cb5-d65bfdb3deaa\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{18}\" :db/id 17592186677189} {:answer/id #uuid \"ff22cf23-2c43-5d63-aaa0-e07be7fbd843\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{54}\" :db/id 17592186677201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"ea5e3f07-99ff-540a-9cb5-d65bfdb3deaa\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{18}\" :db/id 17592186677189} :answer-field/id #uuid \"b6d7a1bb-2b22-5fcb-95fb-9f44edd34200\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677144}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"8d27d9cf-cb74-5840-b365-e7db0c04fb39\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{7}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 9 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=3 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=3,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{3})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(3)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{6}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(3)(\\\\frac{1}{6})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{7}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 9 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 7\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(3 x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 7(\\\\frac{1}{3})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{18}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"3e664b4d-38a8-5ddc-9c88-53307ae3c5bd\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{15}\" :db/id 17592186677085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3e664b4d-38a8-5ddc-9c88-53307ae3c5bd\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{15}\" :db/id 17592186677085} :answer-field/id #uuid \"aa4440e7-6f62-5402-bbe0-f2650309cdb7\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677134}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"a2d73b9e-ded5-5263-8f8f-f603b91d7e83\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{4}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 25 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=5 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=5,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{5})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(5)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(5)(\\\\frac{1}{10})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{4}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 25 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 4\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5 x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 4(\\\\frac{1}{5})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{5}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{15}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"dd1c91ab-c520-5c89-9666-8520331af451\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"dd1c91ab-c520-5c89-9666-8520331af451\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677175} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c9da6c61-240b-51b8-87c9-710e48d7bbfa\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677157}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"a3b89a57-caff-5fb6-834c-90a2ee3c5896\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{7}{\\\\sqrt{9 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{9 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{9(1-(\\\\frac{1}{9})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{3}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{3},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(3)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{3})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{3})(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{7}{\\\\sqrt{9 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{x}{3})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7}{3}(3)\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 7\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 7[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 7(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= 7((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{7 \\\\pi}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"5802f692-c0cd-5a7e-897b-1371d250ef1e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{24}\" :db/id 17592186677093} {:answer/id #uuid \"80d3531a-3427-5af4-ba44-51c4c3208105\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"12 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677111} {:answer/id #uuid \"c7a337d6-4b80-5385-bb22-efe782a80c7b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{8 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677155} {:answer/id #uuid \"cdc8d03a-17cc-5e50-9b8a-ab499d73cb11\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677165} {:answer/id #uuid \"d36c10cf-131b-534c-8641-5d5e441d82eb\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"d36c10cf-131b-534c-8641-5d5e441d82eb\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677169} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c0d68d01-8a42-57ff-b972-69003f4877c2\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"a497f451-04d7-5c60-a172-42abdf02370f\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{8}{36 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{6})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(6)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{6}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(6)(\\\\frac{1}{6})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{8}{36 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 8\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(6 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 8(\\\\frac{1}{6})\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{4}{3}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"584e96b6-767c-5e14-899f-41a99d429ed3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{25 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677094} {:answer/id #uuid \"78ee06fb-d3bd-5699-be5d-99abba240a16\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677108} {:answer/id #uuid \"b534102c-0a28-5ffc-8498-8c551bd0cc23\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677141} {:answer/id #uuid \"ca8e45df-9845-509b-a95f-6c7724735b22\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\pi}{12}\" :db/id 17592186677158} {:answer/id #uuid \"cd0beb08-add5-50bf-82af-696f8e97dadb\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"b534102c-0a28-5ffc-8498-8c551bd0cc23\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\" :db/id 17592186677141} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c2f94dbd-24b0-502c-8bef-8dd471a94ea1\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"a4a4dc8e-8a02-5ef0-a5ce-5526bba8d539\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2}}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{5}{\\\\sqrt{36 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{36 - 6 x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{36(1-(\\\\frac{1}{6})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{6}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\sqrt{6})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6})(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2})=\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\sqrt{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6})(\\\\sqrt{3})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2}}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{5}{\\\\sqrt{36 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{6}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2}}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{6})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{6}(\\\\sqrt{6})\\\\int_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{\\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6}}{6}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6}}{6}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{72}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2a0125b7-63f0-5ffe-9867-939dcd35b2af\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"2a0125b7-63f0-5ffe-9867-939dcd35b2af\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677070} :answer-field/id #uuid \"e3ea9054-3361-5bd8-b506-dcb0dcac388a\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"a7b41060-9701-5ac8-9406-41c43c4a9895\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\frac{9}{81 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{9})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(9)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{9}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(9)(\\\\frac{1}{9})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\frac{9}{81 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 9\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(9 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 9(\\\\frac{1}{9})\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 1\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 1[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= 1(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= 1((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"5d602eba-c683-5422-ab3f-d225478a529e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"5d602eba-c683-5422-ab3f-d225478a529e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677098} :answer-field/id #uuid \"56821200-ef2e-57da-b433-e28f4b7b7183\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677092}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"b68941c7-a358-52a8-9847-bc887dddfa00\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{7}{2}}^{\\\\frac{7}{2}} \\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{49(1-(\\\\frac{1}{49})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{7}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{7},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(7)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- \\\\frac{7}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{7})(- \\\\frac{7}{2})=- \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{7}{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{7})(\\\\frac{7}{2})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- \\\\frac{7}{2}}^{\\\\frac{7}{2}} \\\\frac{6}{\\\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{7}{2}}^{\\\\frac{7}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{x}{7})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{6}{7}(7)\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 6\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 6[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 6(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(- \\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= 6((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= 2 \\\\pi\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"193240cb-9bf1-5fc3-88c4-343eb2b3f61c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{256}\" :db/id 17592186677056} {:answer/id #uuid \"2303c723-3669-5376-b718-f0b6719d1697\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{288}\" :db/id 17592186677061} {:answer/id #uuid \"3e412948-a45d-5098-9434-a82895c83f16\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{96}\" :db/id 17592186677084} {:answer/id #uuid \"b3fa66f7-e082-5e62-acfc-3e26bc6ee6e1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{32}\" :db/id 17592186677139} {:answer/id #uuid \"b40c90ad-24fd-5084-80a0-31c0f725200f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{2048}\" :db/id 17592186677140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3e412948-a45d-5098-9434-a82895c83f16\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{96}\" :db/id 17592186677084} :answer-field/id #uuid \"a7792e2b-87fe-5b28-8d18-1c100112100d\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"b7e7efd8-4daf-5a99-9901-08530da7dbd2\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{\\\\frac{8}{3}}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{3}{3 x^{2} + 64}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n3 x^{2} + 64=64(1+(\\\\frac{3}{64})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} x}{8}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=\\\\frac{8}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8})(\\\\frac{8}{3})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8})(\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3})=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{\\\\frac{8}{3}}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{3}{3 x^{2} + 64}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{64}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{8}{3}}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} x}{8})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{64}(\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\\\int_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{8}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\pi}{96}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"1af8cc77-0bac-5940-b352-f949e5f99a1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{15}\" :db/id 17592186677057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"1af8cc77-0bac-5940-b352-f949e5f99a1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{15}\" :db/id 17592186677057} :answer-field/id #uuid \"0ee451c8-40b1-5552-b4a2-16770a8b9dcd\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677050}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"b8833921-cd55-5d33-86f3-5cb87a7b71f5\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 25 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=5 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=5,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{5})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(5)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{10}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(5)(\\\\frac{1}{10})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 25 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 2\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(5 x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 2(\\\\frac{1}{5})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{5}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{5}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{5}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2}{5}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{15}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"1de17d22-ce4a-560a-97da-52aa819f6d27\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"1de17d22-ce4a-560a-97da-52aa819f6d27\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677058} :answer-field/id #uuid \"8b06cdcb-17e2-5614-8223-3a8a95be3cd8\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"b8ad7c5d-7e92-54a2-a9ba-2d3f25475100\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 64}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\nx^{2} + 64=64(1+(\\\\frac{1}{64})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{8}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{8},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(8)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{8})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{8})(\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 64}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{32}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{8 \\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{x}{8})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{32}(8)\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{4}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2759c9ed-11ea-57ee-a0bd-9ad257122f12\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"2759c9ed-11ea-57ee-a0bd-9ad257122f12\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{5}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677065} :answer-field/id #uuid \"8fcc298a-078c-5c60-b6a2-c6d4172da034\" :answer-field/key \"coefficient\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"ccc64ea2-8f5a-54f2-9e68-90e50c57bc59\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2\" :db/id 17592186677163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"ccc64ea2-8f5a-54f2-9e68-90e50c57bc59\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"2\" :db/id 17592186677163} :answer-field/id #uuid \"e5f1fd37-720c-5104-aaf1-071e70b68d8c\" :answer-field/key \"argument\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677188}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"d5ffb59e-2be0-5b32-8138-13035a607668\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\frac{5}{81 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{coefficient}} $\\\\operatorname{arctan}($ {{argument}} $)$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{9})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(9)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{2}{9}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(9)(\\\\frac{2}{9})=2\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\frac{5}{81 x^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 5\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(9 x)^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 5(\\\\frac{1}{9})\\\\int_{0}^{2} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{9}\\\\int_{0}^{2} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{9}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{9}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(2)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5}{9}((\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(2 \\\\right)})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{5 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(2 \\\\right)}}{9}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"094fa516-728f-5be6-a4fe-f67ece9b8145\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"094fa516-728f-5be6-a4fe-f67ece9b8145\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677047} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ee3d0bc0-f989-584a-ae0f-e17e36f20d73\" :answer-field/key \"answer\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186677190}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"d97ef683-6715-5e81-a7d1-14b2ffc5e533\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{2}}{4}} \\\\frac{9}{\\\\sqrt{9 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\\n\\n{{answer}}\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{9 - 6 x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{9(1-(\\\\frac{2}{3})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{3}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})(- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4})=- \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{2}}{4}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3})(\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{2}}{4})=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{2}}{4}} \\\\frac{9}{\\\\sqrt{9 - 6 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 3\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{4}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{2}}{4}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{3})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 3(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6}}{2}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6}}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(- \\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6}}{2}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3 \\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{4}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"3879e69e-611a-5e48-af0d-b81d5085eba7\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{288}\" :db/id 17592186677081} {:answer/id #uuid \"3c73aab7-f56a-5357-b2cd-6c75dfe0e5f4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{24}\" :db/id 17592186677083} {:answer/id #uuid \"40638152-c07a-5845-b659-ed194dc93945\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677086} {:answer/id #uuid \"48f997ef-3952-5d82-987d-59ddad9a4ab0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677088} {:answer/id #uuid \"fb5a079d-39a5-5990-8353-75942f5fb50c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{4}\" :db/id 17592186677195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"40638152-c07a-5845-b659-ed194dc93945\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677086} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f3205561-4844-5231-9a76-4b12f71b281e\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677191}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"e13e5fe8-8cb3-5e53-84d4-dc9d70dae4ca\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{6} \\\\frac{3}{x^{2} + 36}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\nx^{2} + 36=36(1+(\\\\frac{1}{36})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{6}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{6},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(6)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{6})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=6&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{6})(6)=1\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{6} \\\\frac{3}{x^{2} + 36}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{12}\\\\int_{0}^{6} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{x}{6})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{12}(6)\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\int_{0}^{1} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{2}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{8}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"168d4392-e6c0-51c7-a965-acadc2514a1c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{3 \\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\right)}}{8}\" :db/id 17592186677055} {:answer/id #uuid \"5f917a32-7963-5d6f-afcc-c9a117beb131\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{16}\" :db/id 17592186677099} {:answer/id #uuid \"8b30ca6b-ec84-5558-804b-cffd2d66e216\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{16}\" :db/id 17592186677114} {:answer/id #uuid \"ced38559-d73a-5251-a64c-bdd66fd6d35a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\" :db/id 17592186677166} {:answer/id #uuid \"dbaa1cec-9fad-5c4c-9b41-008695f4e480\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{128}\" :db/id 17592186677174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"8b30ca6b-ec84-5558-804b-cffd2d66e216\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{16}\" :db/id 17592186677114} :answer-field/id #uuid \"7f5c43e5-3673-5b00-8930-97762031a8dc\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677109}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"e1c5c6fb-e670-586e-8a39-d5d94b3f065f\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 64 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\frac{1}{8})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=\\\\frac{1}{16}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(\\\\frac{1}{16})=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\frac{3}{\\\\sqrt{1 - 64 x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 3\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(8 x)^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= 3(\\\\frac{1}{8})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{8}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{8}[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{8}(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{3}{8}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{16}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"2afd7b19-eca2-5ee6-b883-ba3c32d3c286\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677071} {:answer/id #uuid \"33b3be47-676e-5ece-9ce1-37cd7674fbcd\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677074} {:answer/id #uuid \"3684568f-217d-59ef-b6af-389c35869a89\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{18}\" :db/id 17592186677080} {:answer/id #uuid \"759e5427-78c7-5cb9-aa97-0a52c8ecd3d1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677105} {:answer/id #uuid \"77ebe17d-2513-5cde-ae28-8040113f8496\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"77ebe17d-2513-5cde-ae28-8040113f8496\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677107} :answer-field/id #uuid \"0f025f07-d47b-5049-b3e3-da3e27c754f9\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677051}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"e251937d-16b7-5326-a140-cdc3ea29c25f\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{0}^{3 \\\\sqrt{2}} \\\\frac{2}{x^{2} + 6}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\nx^{2} + 6=6(1+(\\\\frac{1}{6})x^{2})\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{6}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(\\\\sqrt{6})\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6})(0)=0 \\\\\\\\ x=3 \\\\sqrt{2}&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{6})(3 \\\\sqrt{2})=\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{0}^{3 \\\\sqrt{2}} \\\\frac{2}{x^{2} + 6}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{3 \\\\sqrt{2}} \\\\frac{1}{1+(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} x}{6})^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{3}(\\\\sqrt{6})\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}[\\\\operatorname{arctan}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{0}^{\\\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}(\\\\operatorname{arctan}(\\\\sqrt{3})-\\\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6}}{3}((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3})-(0)) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{6} \\\\pi}{9}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186677187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"123e3224-f470-5a1e-9a86-1c2e4cc2a51e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{4 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677053} {:answer/id #uuid \"2227dc7c-2cb7-5aee-aa2f-3b4d2b7a95d2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{9}\" :db/id 17592186677060} {:answer/id #uuid \"8b7753d6-ba04-5328-ac31-33c61adfc066\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"4 \\\\pi\" :db/id 17592186677115} {:answer/id #uuid \"8d780d79-735a-5941-8c9f-4801469c84c9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"- \\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677117} {:answer/id #uuid \"ddae6bea-2729-582d-a153-7fc9cd6edc01\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"ddae6bea-2729-582d-a153-7fc9cd6edc01\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"\\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\" :db/id 17592186677176} :answer-field/id #uuid \"90be1365-ed16-5813-b9ee-ed8cc6b8c2ff\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186677122}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"e4cdc649-5543-52c4-9e7e-a603083141ff\" :question/problem \"Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int_{-3}^{3} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{36 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"First factor the constant in the denominator:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sqrt{36 - x^{2}}=\\\\sqrt{36(1-(\\\\frac{1}{36})x^{2})}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\\\frac{x}{6}.$ Then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{d}u}{\\\\mathrm{d}x}=\\\\frac{1}{6},\\\\qquad \\\\mathrm{d}x=(6)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u.\\n$$\\n\\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}x=-3&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{6})(-3)=- \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\\\\ x=3&\\\\Longrightarrow u=(\\\\frac{1}{6})(3)=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\\n\\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\int \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u=\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}+C.\\n$$\\n\\nSubstitute and evaluate:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}& \\\\int_{-3}^{3} \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{36 - x^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\int_{-3}^{3} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-(\\\\frac{x}{6})^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{1}{3}(6)\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 2\\\\int_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\\\\  &= 2[\\\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\\\left(u \\\\right)}]_{- \\\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\\\\  &= 2(\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\\\frac{1}{2})-\\\\operatorname{arcsin}(- \\\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\\\\  &= 2((\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})-(- \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6})) \\\\\\\\  &= \\\\frac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"}] :knowledge-point/title \"Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions\"}",
