        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=5 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{5}} \\frac{3}{25 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=5 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(5)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{3}{5}$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{1}{5}&\\Longrightarrow u=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{5}} \\frac{3}{25 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{5}\\left(\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}\\left(\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
