      "question": {
        ":question/id": "31e905b6-c251-53f6-8de0-854ce3af6324",
        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=4 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1 - 16 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=4 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=1$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{1}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1 - 16 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
